  "id": 203987977436102,
  "uuid": "453095ca-357c-455d-9624-b0728b1c2d7f",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "336fb16a-29f4-b435-4254-7c7b82e3dd96",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "602c592e-5d6c-07bc-a529-e73eac85f0d1",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Ghias began by tracing Darwin's intellectual journey, culminating in his seminal work, ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "On the Origin of Species",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "italic"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": " (1859). She recounted his expedition aboard the HMS Beagle, where meticulous observations of finches and tortoises in the Galápagos Islands led to his theory of natural selection. \"Darwin's dedication to empirical evidence and critical thinking shaped one of the most transformative scientific theories of our time,\" she noted.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The session delved into the historical and religious context of Darwin's era, highlighting the resistance his ideas faced. Despite opposition, Darwin's findings gained acceptance through extensive fossil evidence, comparative anatomy, and, later, molecular biology. \"Scientific discovery often challenges conventional wisdom, and Darwin's work exemplifies the power of evidence-based reasoning,\" Dr Ghias remarked.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "She also explored the role of Alfred Russel Wallace, who independently conceived the theory of natural selection. Wallace's findings, based on observations from the Malay Archipelago, prompted Darwin to publish his work after two decades of contemplation. \"I rather hate the idea of writing for priority,\" Darwin once admitted, reflecting his internal struggle between competition and scientific integrity.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The session underscored key takeaways for educators, particularly Darwin's habits of curiosity, rigorous observation, and resilience. Dr Ghias encouraged teachers to instill these qualities in students, emphasizing that \"education should nurture inquiry and a willingness to challenge assumptions.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "During the interactive Q&A, participants discussed common misconceptions about Darwin's theory, including the false notion that humans descended from chimpanzees. Dr Ghias clarified that evolutionary science posits a shared ancestor rather than direct descent.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Concluding the session, she shared one of Darwin's poignant reflections: ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "\"A man who dares to waste one hour of time has not discovered the value of life yet.\"",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "italic"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": " The quote served as a reminder of the importance of intellectual curiosity and lifelong learning-values that educators must inspire in their students.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Rapporteur:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": " ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Nasreen Bibi (Student, MPhil in Education, AKU-IED)",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "italic"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
